 ## Subtopics
 
-### GPU Octree Encoding
-A variant focusing on the memory layout of the octree nodes to minimize indirections on the GPU. It stops construction at a level where memory requirements fall below a threshold, creating an indirection pool instead of a single root node. Internal nodes store pointers to children, empty leaves encode distance, and non-empty leaves store indices to leaf data. This reduces memory traffic during traversal [S1, unofficial].
-
-### VCP Leaf Data Compression
-A variant detailing the 32-bit encoding of VCP vectors. It transforms vectors to spherical coordinates, applies a square root to the normalized radius for precision, and packs radius (13 bits), azimuthal angle (9 bits), and polar angle (10 bits) into a single integer. It also sorts leaves to share vectors between adjacent blocks, reducing memory usage [S1, unofficial].
+### [GPU Octree Encoding](10089-gpu-octree-encoding.md)
+This method defines the memory layout and traversal optimization for the implicit octree used in VCP surface ray-casting. It solves the problem of efficient GPU memory access by encoding the entire octree hierarchy into a compact array of 32-bit signed integers, eliminating explicit pointer indirections. It sets itself apart from standard octree implementations by packing internal node children, empty leaf distances, and non-empty leaf indices into a single signed integer per node, enabling a flat, cache-friendly memory layout.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Construction is bottom-up, merging $2^3$ blocks at each level. Dynamic loading is performed in the background using multithreading, not interrupting rendering [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Significantly reduced compared to flat VCP grids by storing VCPs only near the surface and using compression. The indirection pool and shared leaf data further optimize space [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Enables finer resolution by reducing runtime memory requirements. Popping artifacts may occur during subvolume switching but are acceptable [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Low memory footprint, no intersection loss, smooth rendering with trilinear interpolation.
-*   **Limitations:** Popping artifacts, complexity of dynamic loading and subvolume management.
+-   **Time Complexity:** Construction is bottom-up, requiring a single pass over the grid to compute $d_{min}$ and subsequent passes for merging. Dynamic loading adds overhead but is performed in the background.
+-   **Memory Complexity:** Significantly reduced compared to flat VCP grids by storing VCP information only near the surface. Leaf data compression further reduces memory usage to 32 bits per vector.
+-   **Performance:** Enables finer resolution rendering on GPUs with limited memory. Dynamic loading allows adaptive refinement based on camera proximity.
+-   **Strengths:** Low memory footprint, no visual artifacts from intersection loss, adaptive refinement.
+-   **Limitations:** Potential popping artifacts during subvolume switching, complexity in implementation due to dynamic loading and custom data types.
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced in "Vector-to-Closest-Point Octree for Surface Ray-Casting" (2015) as a way to efficiently render VCP surfaces on the GPU. It addresses the high memory cost of storing VCP data for large scenes by introducing hierarchy and dynamic loading [S1, unofficial].
+The VCP Octree Construction method was introduced in the paper "Vector-to-Closest-Point Octree for Surface Ray-Casting" [S1, unofficial]. It builds upon the VCP surface representation, which stores a vector field where each grid point stores a vector to the closest point on the original geometry. The octree structure was developed to address the high memory requirements of storing VCP data for large scenes, enabling efficient surface ray-casting on GPUs.
 
